// error
// #1476: struct-literal FIELD types — the last declared-type slot in the
// language with no assignability check, and so the one that finishes the #1470
// class. Until this landed the self-hosted `bit` compiled `S{ v: "s" }` for an
// `f32` field while the seed rejected it: a live accept/reject divergence, the
// worst shape in the self-hosting effort.
//
// It was left out of #1470 deliberately rather than missed. Checking this slot
// means calling checkExprType on the field's value, which nothing had ever done
// (the interface walk recursed into a field only when its declared type was an
// INTERFACE), and that recompute PANICKED with "index out of range" on all 10
// corpus files reaching stdlib/http. Shipping the check on top of that would
// have traded a silent-acceptance hole for a compiler crash, which is strictly
// worse — so the slot stayed unchecked and the matching cells were held out of
// the generated verdict sweep, with pointers both ways, to keep the gate honest
// about what it did not cover.
//
// THE PANIC WAS A REAL BUG, not a reason to skip the check. `scrutineeTypeArgs`
// tested `isExternDecl(c.tree, decl)` BEFORE checking that `decl` belongs to
// this module. `funcDeclLookup` follows imports, so `decl` can index the
// EXPORTING module's tree — its own doc comment says callers must read it
// against `declOwner(...).tree` — and reading that foreign index against the
// current tree ran past the node array. The trigger in every failing file was
// `TlsTrustStore{ roots: bundled().roots }`: a cross-module call whose result
// feeds a struct-literal field, which is why no single-file reproduction of the
// obvious shapes ever panicked. Reordering the guard to check ownership first
// fixes it; the identical latent bug in the parallel `scrutineeTypeArgNodes`
// was fixed with it. Another instance of the standing rule that a decl node is
// only ever readable against the tree that owns it.
//
// With that fixed the sweep's struct-field cells are live again and it is at
// 350/350 MATCH, 0 MISSING, 0 FALSEPOS.

struct S {
  v: f32,
  n: i64,
  s: string,
}

struct Outer {
  inner: S,
  flag: bool,
}

fn main() {
  // The filed shape: a string initializer for a float field.
  let a = S{ v: "s", n: 1, s: "ok" }

  // Not float-specific — every field type is checked the same way.
  let b = S{ v: 4.5, n: "no", s: 5 }

  // A NESTED literal's fields are checked too: the walk has to reach the inner
  // literal's slots, not just the outer field's own declared type.
  //
  // The outer fields here are deliberately all CORRECT, so this statement
  // yields exactly one diagnostic. A nested literal that is wrong at BOTH
  // levels exposes an ordering disagreement that is real but separate: the seed
  // checks a field's value as part of checking that field, so it reports
  // depth-first in field order, while this validator is a pre-order node walk
  // that reports the outer literal's own fields before descending. Both report
  // the same SET of errors with identical text and spans, only sequenced
  // differently. Pinning that sequence here would encode one compiler's walk
  // order as the specification; it is recorded on #1476 instead.
  let c = Outer{ inner: S{ v: true, n: 1, s: "ok" }, flag: true }

  print("${a.n}${b.n}${c.flag}")
}
